          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-constance-ring-9bfcf6a516241001\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Lorck’s written “Jeg elsker dig virkelig Kristine” (“I really do love you, Kristine”) is principally an emphatic avowal presented as true, reinforced by his claim that he is happiest in her arms. It does not itself clearly undertake a commitment, and there is no evidence that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge rather than a calculated written assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Lorck’s sincerity is doubtful in light of his infidelity, reused love language, and self-serving reassurance, but possible deception does not reduce the utterance’s truth-conditional mode. Its relationship-maintaining effect supplies little or no independent performative force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Lorck originally wrote the utterance to Kristine in a letter, which is subsequently read and quoted by the narrator as Constance examines their correspondence.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lorck uses the avowal while minimizing a rival affair and reassuring the jealous Kristine that the other woman has not taken him away. The wider correspondence portrays him as serially unfaithful, conditionally affectionate, and prone to giving Kristine and Constance the same love phrases. This makes the declaration potentially manipulative or insincere, while leaving its core force that of an asserted avowal rather than a commitment or affective reflex.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I dine Arme har jeg det bedst, det skal du huske og tro er sandt. Jeg elsker dig virkelig Kristine” (“I am happiest in your arms; you must remember that and believe it to be true. I really do love you, Kristine”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit insistence that Kristine believe the claim supports a strongly truth-conditional avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The avowal occurs after Lorck dismisses another affair as “den dumme Historie” and says that the rival has never managed to take him entirely away from Kristine.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance functions as deliberate reassurance in response to jealousy, not as an uncontrolled expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lorck qualifies his praise with “notabene naar du er blid og munter” and tells Kristine not to sulk or shed tears.\",\"supports\":\"The calculated and conditional framing further favors assertion and interpersonal management over expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says Lorck’s letters used love words like those he gave Constance, “stundum endog præcis de selvsamme Udtryk” (“sometimes even precisely the very same expressions”).\",\"supports\":\"Reused formulas and parallel affairs create doubts about sincerity and suggest manipulation, while remaining compatible with truth-conditional utterance force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense, explicit first-person love construction in Amalie Skram’s Constance Ring.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified core occurrence and its grammatical presentation as a direct love avowal embedded in correspondence.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core utterance as a written, emphatic assertion of love. Its possible falsity, manipulation, conditionality, and formulaic reuse are contextual qualifications rather than missing dimensions of core force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied translation was used only as an analytical aid; evidential claims are grounded in the source passage and supplied metadata.\"}"
        }
      ],
